{
  "abstract": "Fix distinct nonzero rational anchors $a,b$ with $ab+1$ a nonzero square, and let $B$ be the conic $s^2=ac+1$, $t^2=bc+1$. On the elliptic curve $Y^2=(X+ab)(X+ac)(X+bc)$ over $B$, we prove that the standard geometric rank-four lattice generated by $P=(0,abc)$, a half of $S=(1,rst)$, and the two strong-anchor halves is primitive for every admissible anchor pair, even when additional independent sections occur. This removes the finite-prime saturation qualification in earlier special-anchor results. A uniform signed-divisor calculation classifies all $16$, $32$, or $64$ halving labels when zero, one, or two strong-anchor halves are included: their geometric component genera are $0,1,3,5,9$, and the extra elliptic covers have degree four. For every rational section and odd prime $\\ell$, a genuinely new full division cover is geometrically connected of degree $\\ell^2$ and genus $5\\ell^2-12\\ell+7$; the already-divisible case has one section and a component of genus one larger. If a first-halving cover is connected with $b_\\kappa$ branch points, every $2^n$-division cover of the same section is connected and has genus $1+5\\cdot2^{2n}-6\\cdot2^n(n+1)+b_\\kappa n2^{n-1}$. In particular an elliptic first-half cover becomes genus $25$ at the next halving. We give complete height, monodromy and ramification proofs, with exact independent controls. The results concern division of existing sections at fixed anchors; they do not classify new independent sections or resolve the rational septuple problem.",
